210. Course Schedule II

Approach: BFS + Topological Sorting (Kahn's Algorithm)

We use the same Kahn's Algorithm to find a valid ordering in which all courses can be completed.

Build an adjacency list and calculate the indegree of every course.

Push all courses having indegree 0 into a queue.

Process the queue using BFS and store the processed courses in a topo vector.

Reduce the indegree of neighbouring courses and push them into the queue when their indegree becomes 0.

After BFS, check whether all courses are included in the topological ordering.

If a cycle exists, return an empty vector. Otherwise, return the topological ordering.

Code

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findOrder(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {
        vector<vector<int>> edge(numCourses);
        vector<int> indegree(numCourses, 0);

        for(const auto& it : prerequisites) {
            edge[it[1]].push_back(it[0]);
            indegree[it[0]]++;
        }

        queue<int> q;

        for(int i = 0; i < indegree.size(); i++) {
            if(indegree[i] == 0)
                q.push(i);
        }

        vector<int> topo;

        while(!q.empty()) {
            int num = q.front();
            topo.push_back(num);
            q.pop();

            for(int neigh : edge[num]) {
                indegree[neigh]--;

                if(indegree[neigh] == 0)
                    q.push(neigh);
            }
        }

        if(topo.size() != numCourses)
            return {};

        return topo;
    }
};

Complexity Analysis

Time Complexity: (O(V+E)) — Every vertex and edge is processed once.

Space Complexity: (O(V+E)) — Adjacency list, indegree array, queue and topological ordering.
